  "stable_key": "n7-lesson-07",
  "position": 3,
  "title": "Graphs of linear equations in two variables",
  "summary": "Build and interpret straight-line graphs for equations of the form ax + by = c using points, intercepts and checks.",
  "outcomes": [
    "7.7"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "n7-lesson-06"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Generate ordered pairs that satisfy a linear equation.",
    "Find and plot x- and y-intercepts.",
    "Use a straight-line graph to read values and verify whether points lie on the line."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-explanation-1",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "An equation describes a line",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A linear equation ax+by=c links two variables. Every ordered pair (x,y) that satisfies it is a point on one straight line. A table of values supplies points, but each row must be checked in the original equation before plotting. The diagram shows 2x + y = 6 drawn through its intercepts (0, 6) and (3, 0), with equal scales on both axes so that the steepness is shown truly."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n7-lesson-07-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-explanation-2",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Intercepts give two efficient points",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At the x-intercept, y=0. At the y-intercept, x=0. If both intercepts are within the plotting window, they provide two points that determine the line. Label scales consistently and extend the line rather than drawing only the segment between the points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-explanation-3",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read and verify graph information",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A graph can estimate one variable from the other, while substitution gives an exact check. A point above or below the drawn line does not satisfy the equation. Rearranging to y=mx+c can reveal gradient and y-intercept, but intercept substitution works without rearrangement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-errors",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not swap the coordinates: the x-intercept is the point where y = 0, written (3, 0), not (0, 3). Draw the line through and beyond the plotted points, not just between them, and use the same scale on both axes when the steepness matters. A point that lies near the drawn line is on the line only if it satisfies the equation exactly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-example-1",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: plot from intercepts",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find the intercepts of 2x + y = 6 and use them to draw the line."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "At the x-intercept, y = 0."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2x=6\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "At the y-intercept, x = 0."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Plot (3, 0) and (0, 6) and draw a straight line through them, extending it beyond both points, as in the diagram."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Both points satisfy the equation: 2(3) + 0 = 6 and 2(0) + 6 = 6. A third point, (1, 4), gives 2 + 4 = 6 and lies on the same line."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-example-2",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: find a missing coordinate",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The point (4, k) lies on 3x + 2y = 20. Find k."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute x = 4 and y = k."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "3(4)+2k=20"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "12+2k=20\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2k=8\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k=4"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The point (4, 4) gives 12 + 8 = 20."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find the y-intercept of 3x+y=8."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "8",
        "canonical_latex": "8",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Set x=0, giving y=8."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-07-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every point (x, y) that satisfies ax + by = c lies on one straight line. Find the x-intercept by setting y = 0 and the y-intercept by setting x = 0."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Plot at least two checked points, draw a straight line through and beyond them, and confirm any point exactly by substitution."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
